  "content": {
    "id": "p11-q9-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Let $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-1}$ for $x\\geq1$.",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p11-q9-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $f^{-1}(x)=x^2+1$ for $x\\geq0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$f^{-1}(x)=x^2+1$ for $x\\geq0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}y&=\\sqrt{x-1}\\end{aligned}$$\nSwap $x$ and $y$:\n$$\\begin{aligned}x&=\\sqrt{y-1}\\\\x^2&=y-1\\\\y&=x^2+1\\end{aligned}$$\nThe range of $f$ is $[0,\\infty)$, which becomes the domain of $f^{-1}$. Therefore $f^{-1}(x)=x^2+1$ for $x\\geq0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 38
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q9-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Find an expression for $(f^{-1})'(x)$ using the formula $(f^{-1})'(x)=\\dfrac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(f^{-1})'(x)=2x$ for $x>0$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}f'(x)&=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-1}}\\qquad(x>1).\\end{aligned}$$\nFor $x>0$,\n$$\\begin{aligned}(f^{-1})'(x)&=\\frac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(x))}\\\\(f^{-1})'(x)&=\\frac{1}{f'(x^2+1)}\\\\(f^{-1})'(x)&=\\frac{1}{\\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{(x^2+1)-1}}}\\\\(f^{-1})'(x)&=2\\sqrt{x^2}\\\\(f^{-1})'(x)&=2x.\\end{aligned}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p11-q9-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Verify your result in part (b) by finding $(f^{-1})'(x)$ directly from the equation.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(f^{-1})'(x)=2x$ for $x>0$, agreeing with part (b).",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}f^{-1}(x)&=x^2+1\\\\(f^{-1})'(x)&=\\frac{d}{dx}(x^2+1)\\\\(f^{-1})'(x)&=2x\\qquad(x>0).\\end{aligned}$$\nThis agrees with part (b). At the endpoint $x=0$, the right-hand derivative is $0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 19
      }
    ]
  },
